  "query_preview": "..(Monday through Saturday)\nIssued at 257 PM EDT SAT SEP 26 2026\n\nThe period begins Monday morning with a mature cyclone located in \nthe vicinity of New York City, while an upper ridge axis extends \nfrom west Texas northeast to the Upper MS Valley. Surface high \npressure ridging extends south from southern Canada down the west\nside of the Appalachians, including across eastern Kentucky. An \nupper level low over Nevada and southern California is progged to \nbe upstream of the upper ridge across the middle of the country. \nModels show this low helping to draw tropical moisture from \nHurricane Polo northeastward into the northwestern Mexico and the \nSouthwest CONUS as the week progresses. \n\nModels show the Southwest CONUS upper low weakening gradually as it \nmoves to the center of the country as the week progresses. \nMeanwhile, northerly upper flow between the downstream upper low and \nupstream ridge gradually transitions to west/southwesterly flow \naloft mid to late week as a new downstream ridge develops over the \nTN/OH Valley, bringing a notable rise in temperatures and humidity. \n\nConfidence in the forecast is fairly high through Thursday, with \nsome differences emerging late week that can primarily be explained \nby differences mainly in the Canadian ensembles showing more \ntroughing across the Great Lakes and Midwest compared to ridging on \nthe GEFS and EPS. With the models trending more towards ridging mid \nweek in most modeling, the onset of low precipitation chances has \nbeen pushed back now to Thursday from the west, with gradually \nincreasing PoPs to 50 percent by the end of the long term Friday \nnight. Meanwhile, temperatures will undergo a fairly abrupt warming \ntrend from Monday through Thursday, peaking in the 80s Thursday, \nbefore a cooling trend begins Friday, with abruptness depending \non which solution verifies. Overnight lows will also rise \nsignificantly from the 40s to begin the week to the 60s for the \nsecond half of the week.",
